<<setup, echo=FALSE>>=
library(survival)
library(hmm)

library(knitr)
opts_chunk$set(tidy=FALSE, fig.keep='none',
               fig.path='compete',fig.width=6,fig.height=4)
options(continue="  ", width=60)
pdf.options(pointsize=10) #text in graph about the same as regular text
@ 

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\chapter{Introduction}
\begin{quote}
Kernighan's Law:  ``Everyone knows that debugging is twice as
hard as writing a program in the first place. So if you're as clever as 
you can be when you write it, how will you ever debug it?''
\end{quote}

Work on my own packages has taught me the wisdom of the above. 
Even more to relevant is ``how will you understand the code when you
look at it a year from now''?
Nevertheless, though I try to be simple, there are parts of the code that
are complex.
Even for the 'simpler' parts, 
I find that any time that a new case arises, months or years later that makes me
need to examine my code (and they always do) the end result will be
3x as many additions to the comments as to the code.

This is an attempt to add more context to critical regions.  There are some 
parts of the code where comments just are not enough, one needs figures, tables
and entire paragraphs of text.
I originally undertook this further documentation using Knuth's ``web of code''
idea. I have used the noweb package to intersperse technical
documentation with the source code for the survival package, and the first
drafts of the mshmm package were done that way.
However, despite its advantages, the uptake of noweb by the R community
in general has been nearly nil. 
It is increasingly clear that no future maintainer will continue the 
work, e.g.,
on the survival github page I have yet to recieve a suggested update that
actually
fixed the .Rnw source file instead of the .R file derived from it.  This means
that I can't merge a suggested change automatically, but have to replicate it
myself.

This package does not use noweb.
Instead, this latex document will contain an index, and the R
source code has comments pointing to the index of the code vignette.
(The R code can't refer to ``figure 3.2 in the vignette'' because figure
numbers and labels change).


\section{Parsing the formulas}
\subsection{Overview}
Here is the transition matrix for a moderately complex model.
We have two latent amyloisis states, and 3 latent cognitive states.

<<qplus>>=
states <- c(outer(paste0("A", 0:1), paste0("C", 0:2), paste, sep=':'), "death")
statedata <- data.frame(state= states,
                        A=c(0,1, 0,1, 0,1, 2),
                        N=c(0,0, 1,1, 2,2, NA),
                        D=c(0,0, 0,0 ,0,0, 1))
statedata
qmat <- qplus(statedata, death= "death")
statefig(cbind(2,2,2,1), qmat, alty=rep(1:2, c(7,8)))
@ 

And here is an example call using this data.
<<echo=TRUE, eval=FALSE>>=
mfit <- hmm(list(Surv(time, death) ~ male,
                 0:death ~ iage
                 A(0):A(1) ~ apoe + ns(iage,4)/ common,
                 N(0):N1(1) + N(1):N(2) ~ iage + cmc),
            marker = list(dx ~ N/ multinomial(pattern= pmat),
                           memory~ N + practice/ gaussian,
                           centiloid + sqrt(p.tau181) ~ A(1:2) /gaussian)
            data= dementia, id=ptnum, qmatrix = qmat, 
            statedata = statedata)
@ 

The first argument of hmm is \code{formula}, as with most modeling functions,
and in this case it is a list. 
The 13 separate transitions (arrows) may each have different predictors.
The first element of the list is the default formula, later elements
augment this for specified transitons.
The marker argument is also a list that maps each marker to the state(s)
for which it is a read-out.

The first goal in parsing this is to create an overall master formula:
Surv(time, death) ~ male + iage + apoe + ns(iage,4) + cmc + dx + memory + 
practice + centiloid + sqrt(p.tau181). This will be used as
an argument to the model.frame() call use to create the analysis data set.
This master formula isn't used again.

After dealing with missing values, the code will
use model.reponse() to obtain y, pull the 3 marker variables into a
\code{ymarker} matrix of responses, and create a second master formula,
without the markers, as the argument to model.matrix.

Given the X matrix and it terms and assign attributes, we can the tackle the
second stage of parsing, and create the tmap and cmap matrices.
The tmap matrix has a row per term, so we need the terms first.

\subsection{First pass}
Step 1 is to break the formula into parts, using \code{parsecovar1} for
the formula and \code{parsemarker1} for the marker list.
  The first formula is
the default formula \code{dformula}, the rest become a 3 component list
containing the left sides, the right sides and the options.
The marker argument becomes a list with 4 components of state, marker,
formula and options.
From this we can create a single master formula that is used to drive 
the model.frame process, which ensures that all of the variables we need
will be found in the model frame. In this case the master formula would have
\code{Surv(time, death)} on the left, and
\code{male + apoe + cmc + male*apoe + years + dx + memory + practice}
on the right.  The terms structure of that master formula is very useful.
Further processing of the formulas is deferred until after the model frame
is in hand.

What if the default formula has sex, and
one of the sub-formulas has -sex in it? 
If we just paste together the character form, then if -sex were in the very last
formula, then sex would not appear in the data frame, and formulas that used
sex would fail.
But we paste together terms.labels attributes, which only contain the terms that
do appear (no - signs) and all is well.
One special case would be if sex appeared in the default formula, and then was
removed from every transition in the formula list: sex would then be in the
data frame though we don't need it. If sex also had missing values you could lose
rows we don't intend to.
This error requires almost malicous intent on the part of the user, so we have
decided not to worry about it.

Should we walk the parse tree of the formula, or convert it to character and use
string manipulations?  The latter looks promising until you see a fragment 
like this:
\code{entry:death + dementia:death~ sex + ns(iage, df=4) / common},
whose parse tree is shown in figure \ref{figparse}.
Walking the parse tree is a bit more subtle, but we then can take advantage of 
all the knowledge built into the R parser.
A formula is a 3 element list of ``~'', leftside, rightside, or 2 elements if 
it has only a right hand side.  Legal ones for hmm have both left and right.

\begin{figure}
<<R, echo=FALSE>>= 
opar <- par(mar=c(1,1,1,1))
ptplot(entry:death + dementia:death~ sex + ns(iage, df=4) / common)
par(opar)
@
  \caption{The parse tree for the formula 
    \code{entry:death + dementia:death~ sex + ns(iage, df=4) / common)}.
    At branches we show the class followed by the value.}
  \label{figparse}
\end{figure}

<<R, ptree>>=
test <- cu:death + dem:death ~ x1 + x2
length(test)
test[[1]]
test[[2]]
test[[3]]

length(test[[3]])
test[[3]][[1]]
test[[3]][[2]]
@ 

Extrapolating from the above, the way one walks a parse tree is to simply
step along the nested list.
Nested functions can be quite efficient, and look simple, but also be
difficult to get ones arms around, and an absolute bear to debug.
Here is a simple function from the parsemarker.R code, whose job it is to
pluck off the leftmost object in the right hand side of a formula, and
return a list containing that object as the first item, and the updated formula
without that item as the second.  
Here is a listing of the function, along with a test formula.
<<r, >>=
grableft <- function(x) {
    if (is.call(x) && x[[1]]== as.name('+')) {
        if (is.name(x[[2]])) list(x[[2]], x[[3]])
        else {
            temp <- grableft(x[[2]])
            x[[2]] <- temp[[2]]
            list(temp[[1]], x)
        }
    } else stop("state identifier can only be followed by '+'")
}

test <- (~ N + practice+ sex)[[2]]
ptplot(test)
@ 

The execution sequence looks like below, each indent a recursive call to 
grableft, executed statements listed seqentially within the indent.
\begin{itemize}
  \item grableft(N + practice + sex)
    \begin{itemize}
      \item temp <- grableft(N + practice)  (+ N practice)
        \begin{itemize}
          \item list(N, practice)
        \end{itemize}
      \item x[[2]] <- temp[[2]] (x = + practice sex)
      \item list(N, practice + sex)
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

One useful ideas is to use the terms() function to much of the work for
us. For the right hand size the resulting \code{term.names} attribute nicely
picks off the variables that should be pasted together to make a master
formula from a list of formulas, which is exactly how we create the master
formula.
However, it doesn't work for something like 1:2 on the left hand side of
a formula, since '1' and '2' are not valid variable names. 
More fatally, terms() considers A:B and B:A to be the same, which means that
both never appear in the same terms object, and which label is used can
be rather arbitrary. In truth an
A$\rightarrow$B transition is not the same as B$\rightarrow$A, and we might
even have both in the same formula line.
Nor does terms() work for splitting off the options, which involves finding the 
last '/' on the line.  So for many of the operations we will need to
walk the tree. 

In the code, 
the parsecovar1 routine accepts the list of formulas and returns 3 lists:
left hand sides, right hand sides (less options), and options, which are the
things like ``common'' and ``init'' that live after the last /.
The list of right hand sides is used to build the master formula,
which is used to create the model frame and the X matrix.

Once the model

The left hand side of each formula specifies the set of transitions to which
the covariates apply, and is more complex.
Say instance that we had 7 states and the following statedata
data set.
\begin{center}
  \begin{tabular}{cccc}
    state & A&  N&  D \\ \hline 
    A0N0 &  0&  0 & 0\\
    A1N0 &  1&  0 & 0\\
    A0N1 &  0&  1 & 0\\
    A1N1 &  1&  1 & 0\\
    A0N2 &  0&  2 & 0\\
    A1N2 &  1&  2 & 0\\
    Death&  2 & NA& 1 
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

  Here are some valid transitions
\begin{enumerate}
   \item 0:state('Death'),   any transition to the death state
   \item 0:Death, same as above
   \item A0N0:D(0), a transition from A0N0, but not to death
   \item A(0):A(1), any transition that starts with A=0 and ends with A=1
   \item N(0):N(1,2) + N(1):N(2), an upward change of N
   \item A0N0:c('A0N1','A1N0'); a pair of states
   \item (1:3 + 2:3):4;  we can refer to states by number
   \item c(1,2):3 is legal, (1,2):3 is not; the second flagged as invalid
     by the R parser
\end{enumerate}



The markers are a somewhat more complex beast.Here are some valid ones
\begin{itemize}
  \item \code{log(amyloid) + log(tau) ~ A(0:1) / gaussian}
  \item \code{pzmemory ~ N + practice / gaussian(param='mean')}
  \item \code{pzmemory ~ N / gaussian(param='std')}
  \item \code{dx ~ state / multinomial(pattern = pmat)}
\end{itemize}

What is going on here? 
\begin{itemize}
 \item The first thing is that in an HMM the value of a marker is
   predicted from the state, not vice versa, thus the marker is coded as the
   response. The state must be the first predictor, and is often the only one. 
   We always treat it as a factor, e.g., an intercept per state.
   ``A'' above says to use the ``A'' column of the state data.  Subjects in the
   A0N0, A0N1 and A0N2 states are assumed to have the same distribution of 
   amyloid.
 \item The specified distribution will have multiple parameters, the mean
   and std for a Gaussian for example. As in lines 2 and 3 we can specify
   different formulae for the mean and std, mean memory scores are influenced 
   both by the true state and a practice effect, but we assumed no practice
   effect for the std.  As a convenience, the default is to use the same
   equation for both (the default is both parameters).
 \item The multinomial distribution requires a pattern matrix which specifies
   the mapping between linear predictors and states.
\end{itemize}

What we must have out of parsemarker1 is the set of terms that will be needed
in the model frame.
These are passed back as the equations which will be used via the term.labels
attribute of a terms() result.
It turns out that putting two things on the left of ~ does not work, they
don't end up as separate character strings in the term.labels attribute.
This complicates the code for the first marker equation somewhat.
The routine also sends back various bits that will be useful for the second
routine.

For the second step of parsing, what we need from parsecovar2 and parsecovar3
is the coefficient map cmap. Given this, there is a simple transformation from
the total coefficent vector \code{coef} to a matrix form B (it will have lots of
zeros) with one column per transition, such that \verbzX%*% Bz gives the
matrix of linear predictors; one row per observation, one column per transition.
A matrix \code{tmap} with one row per term is an intermediate step: a factor 
or ns() term has multiple coefficients.  The term() function (and users) work
with terms, the assign attribute of the X matrix translates this to coefficients.

Assume there were three markers a, b, c.  The likelihood 
for any given subject can be written as
$$\pi_0 \left[D_a(y_{1a}, t_1)D_b(y_{1b}t_1) D_c(y_{1c}, t_1)\right] P(t_1,t_2) 
\left[D_a(y_{2a},t_2) D_b(y_{2b},t_2) D_c(y_{2c},t_2)) \right] P(t_2,t_3)  \ldots, 
\left[D_a(y_{na},t_n) D_b(y_{nb},t_n) D_c(y_{nc}, t_n) \right]
$$
with $pi$ the initial distribution over states,
$P$ an nstate by nstate transition matrix, and $D$ a diagnoal response matrix
for each marker.
Each row of $XB$ for a subject generates a transition matrix $P$ except the
last, and row of data with markers generates a set of $D$ matrices, 
otherwise $D$ is the identity matrix.

For the transition matrices, the overall $XB$ matrix is exactly what we need.
The likelihood computation for an hmm is separate for each subject; the parallel
routine for a subject can be handed the rows of $XB$ for that subject.
For the markers it is useful to think about the compuation piecewise.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item For many data sets, in particular those which treat current age as a
    time-dependent covariate, the will be many rows of data which only update
    age: most rows won't have markers measured.  Even for data rows that 
    correspond to a regular visit, not every marker will bet measured on every
    subject.  Thus it is useful to have a separate
    y vectors for each marker, to avoid spurious NA.
  \item The linear predictor(s) for each parameteter of a distribution/marker 
    pair, e.g., the mean of a gaussian, can be decomposed into two parts:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item The portion due to state, which will be a vector of length nstate,
        idential for all subjects
      \item The portion due to any extra variables, such as \code{practice} in
        the example above.  These can be incorporated into $XB$. Very often
        there will be none.
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}


Start with a simple case. For a gamma distribution there will be 2 parameters
per peak, the mean and std.  The number of peaks is taken from the relevant
column of statedata: 2 for A, 3 for N.  The total number of linear predictors
for the log(tau):A(0:1) mapping will be 4 with names of ``log(tau):mean.A0'',
``log(tau):mean.A1'', ``log(tau):log(std).A0'' and ``log(tau):log(std).A1''.
The density routine will be passed a vector $y$ of log(tau) values, a
4 column matrix of parameters, and the mapping of the two peaks to the 7 states.
It will return an nstate by $m$ matrix of
probabilities where $m$ is the number or rows of $y$.  
The associated cmap will have 4 columns and rows for the intercept and practice.

A multinomial outcome will have labels that are not quite as obvious. Say that
we have the following pattern matrix.
<<pattern>>=
pmat <- rbind(c(-1, 1, 0), c(2, -1, 3), c(0, 4, -1))
dimnames(pmat) <- list(true=c("C0", "C1", "C2"), 
                       obs =c("CU", "MCI", "dementia"))
pmat
@ 

This has declared that we will never mistakenly apply a ``dementia'' label to
someone who is in the C0 state, nor a ``cognitively unimpaired'' label to
one who is in dementia. Thus the multinomial distribution for true C0 has
only 1 parameter (it is binomial) and likewise for C2, C1 has 2 parameters.
The -1 codes the cell that should be thought of as the reference.
In this case labels will be ``diag:eta1'', ``diag:eta2'', ``diag:eta3'', and
``diag:eta4''; there is not a nice 1:1 relationship between any parameter and 
the entries in the probability matrix. 
It is possible, though I would find it odd, to force parameters to be
equal.  A second row of 2, -1, 2 for instance would force the probability of
classifiying true C1 as unimpaired or demented to be identical.

How we handle this is to have a triad of functions for each distribution.
\begin{itemize}
 \item An initialization function is given the number of peaks, e.g., the
relevant column of statedata, along with any optional parameters. It returns
the number of linear predictors along with names for them, along with optional
data to pass to the compuatation function.
 \item The compuatation function recieves the vector y, the matrix of linear
   predictors, and the optional information.  It returns a nstate x n matrix
   of density values.
 \item A reporting function can transform results to a user scale.  This is
   trivial for the gaussian, just turn log(std) into std.  For the multinomial
   produce the misclassification matrix.
\end{itemize}
This allows for some freedom, for instance the gaussian setup could allow an
\code{std=2} argument, which would fix the standard devation for the distribution
at 2. That would also reduce the number of linear predictors.

The user can write either \code{gaussian} or \code{gaussian(param=std)}, as
an example. The parsecovar routine picks up the name, puts the parametes in
a list along with the state data, and calls the initialzation function. The
fact that the code uses log(std) is something the user need not even know.

One important foonote is the use of A(0:1) in the formula, which says that there
is no gaussian peak for death.  For N the use of NA for death accomplishes the
same thing.
If death is the true state, there is no tau measurement, and the associated D 
matrix has a 0 in the last position. 
Put another way, having a tau measurement means that you weren't dead.

One thing that confused me in this process was that if a model has at least
one categorical predictor, it is possible to bundle the death state into
that outcome, e.g., treat CU/MCI/dementia/death as the categorical outcome.
That is how some examples in the msm documentation are set up. One can then
set the pattern matrix for the multinomial to have a final row ``true death''
and final colum ``observed death'' that are both 0 except for a lower right
corner of 1.  In the hmm function death is handled via the status variable
instead, and adding a death to the in-office-diagnosis would be a mistake.
(This came to a head the first time I had an msm model without any categorical
markers and wondered ``where do I put death''.)
In an hmm, we computation at any given time point purposely does death last,
to accomodate the admittedly strange case of a subject who had a clinical 
evaluation in the morning and died in the afternoon.



